window.docContents[111].resolve({"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out--Small-rigorous-improvement":{"contents":"* Question* Context change* Attribute change* Risk shown* PhysLib source\n* The oscillator loses energy. What must the model add, and how should it distinguish the damper's measurement from the whole oscillator's decay?* Add a damper to the oscillator and represent the whole as a nominally named DampedRig.* γ is measured on the damper and m on the body; both retain the whole oscillator's bearer. ISO damping coefficient δ and the damping regime belong to the whole.* Bare γ < ω and δ + ω₀ compile despite wrong or distinct kinds; cross-system division and mixing A's and B's states are refused.* DampedHarmonicOscillator.Basic; ISO 80000-3 items 3-18, 3-24, and 3-25.\n\nL0–L4's conservation assumption ends here. The PhysLib dissipation law says that the\nenergy derivative is negative when the damper is active and the velocity is nonzero.\nThis rung is optional; the requirements lesson remains L6.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L5 — The oscillation dies out","header":"5.6.5. Small rigorous improvement","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out--Small-rigorous-improvement"}});